%I A400864 #29 Nov 01 2026 21:13:07
%S A400864 1,2025,21600,36000,48600,67200,86400,105600,124800,163200,182400,
%T A400864 220800,225000,278400,297600,302400,355200,393600,412800,451200,
%U A400864 504000,508800,566400,585600,643200,681600,700800,725760,758400,796800,854400,931200,969600,988800
%N A400864 Subsequence of A036438 numbers of the form W = k * d(k), where both k and W are refactorable (A033950), and k satisfies d(k/d(k)) = d(d(k)) where d(x) is the divisor count of x.
%C A400864 Is a subsequence of A400859 that requires the product W be refactorable, if W is also required to meet the divisor constraint of the quotient k in A400859, d(k/d(k)) = d(d(k)) then 36000, and 48600 are the first two nontrivial terms to also satisfy d(W/d(W)) = d(d(W)).
%C A400864 The d(k) = 30 is notable for having 6 finite iterations in A400859; 21600, 36000, 48600, 121500. 225000, and 337500. Among them only 21600, 36000, 48600, and 225000 are refactorable appearing in A400864.
%D A400864 Tom M. Apostol, Introduction to Analytic Number Theory, Springer-Verlag, 1976, pp. 29 ff. James J. Tattersall, Elementary Number Theory in Nine Chapters, Cambridge University Press, 1999, page 162.
%D A400864 Richard K. Guy, Unsolved Problems in Number Theory, 3rd Edition, Springer, 2004, Section B12, pp. 102-103. New Scientist, Sep 05 1998, p. 17, para. 3.
%D A400864 S. Ramanujan, Collected Papers, Ed. G. H. Hardy et al., Cambridge 1927; Chelsea, NY, 1962.
%e A400864 21600 is a term because 720*d(720) = 21600 since d(720/30) = d(24) = 8 = d(30) and both 720 and 21600 are refactorable.
%o A400864 (PARI) isok(n) = fordiv (n, k, my(dk=numdiv(k)); if ((k*dk == n) && !(n%numdiv(n)) && !(k%dk) && (numdiv(k/dk) == numdiv(dk)), return (1))); \\ _Michel Marcus_, Oct 10 2026
%Y A400864 Cf. A000005, A036438, A400850.
%Y A400864 Intersection of A033950 and A400859.
%K A400864 nonn,new
%O A400864 1,2
%A A400864 _Maxwell C. P. Brown_, Oct 07 2026
